<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<config>
	<!--
	  The switches behind the drone's "Automation modules" talk menu (see dialogs.xml).

	  WHY BUFFS AT ALL. The menu has to change server-side state from a click in a client-side UI,
	  and this mod ships no client DLL. The dialog system's only extensible write is
	  <action type="AddBuff"/> (BaseDialogAction.ActionTypes has exactly six members, and AddBuff is
	  the only one that isn't quest/trader/voice plumbing). So each menu row adds a buff that writes
	  one CVar, and DronePatch reads that CVar.

	  The round trip is what makes it work without a client mod:
	    - the client's own EntityBuffs.AddBuff runs the effect locally, so the menu row flips the
	      instant you click it;
	    - AddBuff net-syncs, and NetPackageAddRemoveBuff.ProcessPackage re-applies the buff on the
	      server, so the server runs the same ModifyCVar and DronePatch reads the result.
	  Nothing is polled and no custom NetPackage exists, so a vanilla client cannot be kicked over it.

	  *** WHY onSelfBuffStack AND NOT onSelfBuffStart. ***
	  This is the whole reason these buffs are permanent, and it is not obvious.

	  EntityBuffs.AddBuff, on a buff the entity does NOT already have, only appends a BuffValue to
	  ActiveBuffs. It fires NOTHING. onSelfBuffStart is raised later, when the buff first ticks.
	  But the dialog redraws its row list exactly once per click - XUiC_DialogResponseList.Update
	  rebuilds only while IsDirty, and OnPressResponse is the only thing that sets it - and a row's
	  requirement is evaluated only in the XUiC_DialogResponseEntry.CurrentResponse setter, during
	  that rebuild. So a CVar written on buff start lands AFTER the only redraw, and every row shows
	  the state from the previous click: click once, nothing appears to happen; click again and you
	  see the first click's result. That was the 0.7.5 behaviour and it looked like dropped clicks.

	  The stacking branch of AddBuff is different: when the buff IS already active it calls
	  FireEvent(onSelfBuffStack, ...) inline, before AddBuff returns. So the CVar is already written
	  by the time SelectResponse switches the statement, and the rebuild that follows sees it.

	  Hence: duration 0 (infinite - BuffClass.Tick only expires a buff while DurationMax > 0), the
	  effect hung on onSelfBuffStack, and DronePatch.PrimeToggleBuffs keeping every one of them present
	  on the owner so that every click is a stack. Priming is inert precisely BECAUSE there is no
	  onSelfBuffStart effect - adding one back would make the drone's own tick flip the player's
	  switches. stack_type is stated explicitly for the same reason: only replace/duration/effect
	  fire the event, and "ignore" would silently make every row dead.

	  POLARITY IS DELIBERATE. The CVar means DISABLED, not enabled. EntityBuffs.GetCustomVar returns
	  0 for a name it has never seen and EntityBuffs.Write skips any CVar sitting at 0, so "absent"
	  and "zero" have to be the safe state - otherwise every existing player's modules would go dark
	  on update, and switching one back on would leave nothing on disk to prove it.

	  OPERATION IS ALWAYS "set". On a dedicated server a buff applied to a player can fire both
	  onSelfBuffStart and onSelfBuffStack for one application, and the server's own re-apply echoes
	  back to the client, so any relative op (add/subtract) applies more than once. "set" is
	  idempotent, so no amount of echo can corrupt the flag.
	-->
	<append xpath="/buffs">

		<!-- Auto-Loot -->
		<buff name="buffDaLootDisable" hidden="true">
			<stack_type value="replace"/>
			<duration value="0"/>
			<effect_group>
				<triggered_effect trigger="onSelfBuffStack" action="ModifyCVar" cvar="daLootOff" operation="set" value="1"/>
			</effect_group>
		</buff>
		<buff name="buffDaLootEnable" hidden="true">
